Day 9: Unsupervised Machine Learning: Clustering & Dimensionality Reduction

So far (Days 7 and 8) every model had labels to learn from. Today there are no labels: we look for structure in the data itself.

1. Supervised vs Unsupervised Learning
	Supervised	Unsupervised
Data	Features + labels (X, y)	Features only (X)
Goal	Predict a known target	Discover hidden structure
Examples	Regression, classification	Clustering, dimensionality reduction
Evaluation	Compare to true labels	Harder: no ground truth

Common uses: customer segmentation, anomaly detection, topic discovery, data compression, visualization, preprocessing for other models.

2. K-Means Clustering

Idea: Split data into k clusters, each represented by its centroid (mean point).

Algorithm

Choose k and initialize k centroids.
Assign each point to its nearest centroid.
Update each centroid to the mean of its assigned points.
Repeat steps 2 and 3 until assignments stop changing.

Objective: minimize inertia (WCSS, within-cluster sum of squares): Σ ‖xᵢ − μ_cluster‖²

Important points

Feature scaling is mandatory (distance-based, like KNN).
Results depend on initialization. Use k-means++ (the default) and n_init=10 or more.
You must choose k in advance.
Assumes clusters are roughly spherical and similar in size. It struggles with elongated or irregular shapes.
Sensitive to outliers (they pull centroids).

Choosing k

Elbow method: plot inertia vs k and look for the “bend”. Inertia always decreases as k grows, so look for diminishing returns.
Silhouette score: s = (b − a) / max(a, b), where a = mean distance to points in own cluster, b = mean distance to the nearest other cluster. Ranges from −1 to 1. Higher is better.
Also use domain knowledge: does the number of segments make business sense?
3. Hierarchical Clustering
Agglomerative (bottom-up): start with every point as its own cluster and repeatedly merge the two closest clusters.
Divisive (top-down): start with one cluster and split. Rarely used.
Dendrogram: a tree showing the merge order. Cut it at a chosen height to get clusters.
Linkage (how cluster distance is defined):
ward: minimizes variance increase (default, works well with Euclidean distance)
complete: maximum distance between points
average: mean distance between points
single: minimum distance (can cause “chaining”)
Pros: no need to fix k upfront, and the dendrogram is informative.
Cons: slow on large datasets (at least O(n²)), and merges can’t be undone.
4. DBSCAN (Density-Based Clustering)

Idea: Clusters are dense regions separated by sparse regions.

Parameters

eps: neighborhood radius
min_samples: minimum points within eps to count as dense

Point types

Core point: has at least min_samples neighbors within eps
Border point: within eps of a core point but not core itself
Noise point: neither (labeled -1)

Pros: finds clusters of any shape, detects outliers automatically, and you don’t specify k.
Cons: struggles when clusters have very different densities, is sensitive to eps, and degrades in high dimensions. Scaling is required.

Tip: choose eps using a k-distance plot (sort each point’s distance to its k-th neighbor and look for the elbow).

	K-Means	Hierarchical	DBSCAN
Need k upfront?	Yes	No (cut dendrogram)	No
Cluster shape	Spherical	Depends on linkage	Arbitrary
Handles outliers?	Poorly	Poorly	Yes (noise label)
Scales to large data?	Yes	No	Moderately
5. Evaluating Clusters
Internal metrics (no labels needed):
Silhouette score: higher is better
Davies–Bouldin index: lower is better
Inertia: only for comparing the same algorithm at different k
External metrics (if true labels exist, e.g., for practice):
Adjusted Rand Index (ARI): 1 = perfect match, ~0 = random
Always interpret clusters: compute the mean of each feature per cluster and describe what makes each group distinct.
6. Dimensionality Reduction

Why reduce dimensions?

Visualize high-dimensional data in 2D or 3D
Remove noise and redundant (correlated) features
Speed up training and reduce overfitting
Fight the curse of dimensionality (distances become less meaningful as dimensions grow)
Principal Component Analysis (PCA)

Idea: Find new axes (principal components) that are linear combinations of the original features and capture the maximum variance. They are orthogonal (uncorrelated) to each other.

Steps

Standardize the data.
Compute the covariance matrix.
Find eigenvectors (directions) and eigenvalues (variance along each).
Sort by eigenvalue and keep the top components.
Project the data onto them.

Key concepts

Explained variance ratio: the share of total variance each component captures.
Cumulative explained variance: pick the number of components that retains about 90–95%.
PCA(n_components=0.95) selects this automatically.
PCA is unsupervised and linear. Components are harder to interpret than the original features.
Scaling is mandatory, otherwise large-scale features dominate.
Fit PCA on the training data only, then transform the test data (avoid leakage).
t-SNE and UMAP (introduction only)
Non-linear methods used mainly for visualization of clusters in 2D.
t-SNE: distances between clusters and cluster sizes in the plot are not reliable. Don’t use it as a preprocessing step for models.
UMAP: faster than t-SNE and better at preserving global structure.
7. Practical Implementation
python
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.datasets import load_wine, make_moons
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.cluster import KMeans, AgglomerativeClustering, DBSCAN
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA
from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score, adjusted_rand_score

# 1. Load and scale data (we ignore the labels while clustering)
X, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)
X_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)

# 2. Choose k: elbow method + silhouette score
ks = range(2, 11)
inertias, sils = [], []
for k in ks:
    km = KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=42).fit(X_scaled)
    inertias.append(km.inertia_)
    sils.append(silhouette_score(X_scaled, km.labels_))

fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 4))
ax[0].plot(ks, inertias, marker="o"); ax[0].set_title("Elbow (Inertia)")
ax[1].plot(ks, sils, marker="o");     ax[1].set_title("Silhouette Score")
for a in ax: a.set_xlabel("k")
plt.show()

# 3. Fit final K-Means and compare with the true labels (for learning only)
kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=42).fit(X_scaled)
print("K-Means silhouette:", round(silhouette_score(X_scaled, kmeans.labels_), 3))
print("K-Means ARI       :", round(adjusted_rand_score(y, kmeans.labels_), 3))

# 4. Hierarchical clustering
agg = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=3, linkage="ward").fit(X_scaled)
print("Hierarchical ARI  :", round(adjusted_rand_score(y, agg.labels_), 3))

# 5. DBSCAN on non-spherical data (K-Means would fail here)
X_moons, _ = make_moons(n_samples=300, noise=0.07, random_state=42)
db = DBSCAN(eps=0.2, min_samples=5).fit(X_moons)
print("DBSCAN clusters:", len(set(db.labels_) - {-1}),
      "| noise points:", (db.labels_ == -1).sum())

# 6. PCA: how many components do we need?
pca = PCA().fit(X_scaled)
cum_var = np.cumsum(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)
print("Components for 95% variance:", np.argmax(cum_var >= 0.95) + 1)

plt.plot(range(1, len(cum_var) + 1), cum_var, marker="o")
plt.axhline(0.95, color="red", linestyle="--")
plt.xlabel("Number of components"); plt.ylabel("Cumulative explained variance")
plt.show()

# 7. Visualize clusters in 2D using PCA
X_2d = PCA(n_components=2).fit_transform(X_scaled)
plt.scatter(X_2d[:, 0], X_2d[:, 1], c=kmeans.labels_, cmap="viridis", s=30)
plt.xlabel("PC1"); plt.ylabel("PC2"); plt.title("K-Means clusters in PCA space")
plt.show()

# 8. Interpret the clusters
df = pd.DataFrame(X, columns=load_wine().feature_names)
df["cluster"] = kmeans.labels_
print(df.groupby("cluster").mean().round(2).T)

What to observe

The elbow and silhouette plots should both point towards k = 3 for this dataset.
Without scaling, K-Means gives noticeably worse results. Try removing the scaler.
On the moons data, K-Means would cut the shapes in half, while DBSCAN recovers them.
Fewer PCA components than the original 13 features keep most of the variance.
